."]], "related": ["/ontology-knowledge-graph/", "/fractional-cto/", "/ai-architect/"], "citations": [{"title": "AI features and your website", "url": "https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features", "publisher": "Google Search Central"}, {"title": "Introduction to structured data markup in Google Search", "url": "https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/intro-structured-data", "publisher": "Google Search Central"}, {"title": "Microsoft Bing/Copilot use schema for its LLMs", "url": "https://searchengineland.com/microsoft-bing-copilot-use-schema-for-its-llms-453455", "publisher": "Search Engine Land", "date": "Mar 2025"}, {"title": "Schema.org", "url": "https://schema.org/", "publisher": "Schema.org"}, {"title": "IndexNow", "url": "https://www.indexnow.org/", "publisher": "IndexNow"}]}-->
<p>When I rebuilt this site, I added structured data to every page: a Person and services on the home page, articles with authors and dates on every insight, breadcrumbs and FAQs. Clients often ask whether that is how you get into ChatGPT, Gemini or Copilot answers. The honest answer is: it helps machines understand you, and that's worth doing, but it is not a switch.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What the search engines have said</h2>
<p><strong>Google.</strong> Its guidance on AI features says the same foundational SEO practices apply to AI Overviews and AI Mode, including making sure structured data matches the visible content on the page<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Google's general documentation describes structured data as a way to help it understand page content and to qualify for rich results<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. It doesn't promise AI citations in return.</p>
<p><strong>Microsoft.</strong> Search Engine Land reported that Bing's Fabrice Canel said at SMX Munich in March 2025 that schema markup helps Microsoft's LLMs understand content, and suggested IndexNow for getting fresh content discovered<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>Others.</strong> The same report noted it was unclear whether other AI engines use schema at all<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Claims of large citation lifts from markup alone mostly come from vendors and are hard to verify.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Treat structured data as making your site unambiguous to machines, not as a ranking hack. Unambiguous is valuable; it just isn't magic.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Think of it as a small ontology</h2>
<p>Schema.org is a shared vocabulary of types and properties<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, and JSON-LD on your pages forms a small knowledge graph about you. The same principles I use in enterprise <a href="/insights/ontology-vs-knowledge-graph-vs-semantic-layer/">ontology work</a> apply:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stable identities.</strong> Give the person or organisation a single identifier and reference it from every page, so authorship and services all point to the same entity.</li>
<li><strong>Explicit relationships.</strong> Articles have an author; services have a provider; pages sit in a breadcrumb hierarchy. Say so in the markup.</li>
<li><strong>Consistency with what people see.</strong> Markup that claims something the page doesn't show is a data quality failure, and Google's guidance says to keep them matched<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Validation.</strong> Check markup on every build, just as I would validate a knowledge graph with <a href="/insights/shacl-validation-knowledge-graphs/">SHACL shapes</a>.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I put on a professional services site</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Page</th><th>Types</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Home</td><td>Person or Organization, WebSite, ProfessionalService</td><td>Who you are, what you offer, where you work</td></tr>
<tr><td>Service pages</td><td>Service, BreadcrumbList, FAQPage where visible</td><td>Each offering as a distinct, linked entity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Articles</td><td>BlogPosting with author, dates and publisher</td><td>Authorship and freshness, tied to the same person</td></tr>
<tr><td>Index pages</td><td>Blog or CollectionPage</td><td>How content is organised</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Alongside markup, a few unglamorous things matter more than any schema type: fast pages, clear headings, accurate dates, a sitemap, and pushing new URLs to search engines promptly. This site uses IndexNow, an open protocol for notifying participating search engines of changes<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>, triggered whenever the sitemap changes.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What actually earns AI citations</h2>
<p>Nobody outside the AI companies knows exactly how their systems choose sources, and anyone who claims certainty is selling something. What is reasonable to expect: content that answers a specific question clearly, cites its own sources, is attributed to an identifiable expert and is easy to parse will do better than vague content, whatever the markup. Structured data supports that; it doesn't replace it.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The enterprise version of the same problem</h2>
<p>Inside companies, the same question appears as "how do we make our data understandable to AI?" The answer is the same at larger scale: shared vocabularies, stable identifiers, explicit relationships and validation. That is the work behind a governed <a href="/ontology-knowledge-graph/">ontology and knowledge graph</a>, and it is what makes retrieval for AI trustworthy.</p>
</section>

